        <h2>1. Grip Strength and Forearm Endurance</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: training principle, not a dead hang trial. Hanging holds the forearm flexors under sustained isometric load, so grip endurance is the most direct thing a hang trains. Deadlifts, rows, pull-ups and carries all end when the hands open first, and grip endurance helps there.</p>
        <p>Dead hangs work the flexor digitorum profundus, flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor pollicis longus together. Fingertip push-ups and wrist curls work these muscles in different ways, so hangs are one option among several. The <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength training guide</a> covers forearm programming in detail.</p>
        <p>A common starting point is 10 to 20 second holds, adding about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <h2>2. The Spine</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: mechanism only. Hanging unloads the spine while you hang. No study has measured disc height or pressure during a bodyweight hang, and a Cochrane review (Wegner et al., 2013) found traction gives little or no benefit for low back pain. Some people find short hangs ease stiffness.</p>
        <p>People are a little shorter in the evening than in the morning, and no study shows that a hang reverses that. Short holds of 10 to 30 seconds are a common starting dose, and no trial defines a minimum effective time. The <a href="/spine-decompression/">spine and dead hangs guide</a> sets out what is and is not known.</p>

        <h2>3. Shoulder Mobility</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: one surgeon's proposal, not tested. Orthopedic surgeon John M. Kirsch proposed overhead hanging for shoulder impingement in a self-published book (5th ed. 2019). No published randomized trial has tested it (Europe PMC search, October 2026).</p>
        <p>A hang puts the arms overhead. People commonly feel a stretch through the chest and the underside of the shoulder, but this is not measured in any study cited here.</p>
        <p>Not all shoulder pain responds to hanging, and rotator cuff tendinopathy can feel worse with hanging. Whether pain improves with hanging does not identify its cause, so see a clinician for a diagnosis. Start with short holds and stop if pain increases. The <a href="/shoulder-health/">shoulder health guide</a> has the details and the situations to avoid.</p>

        <h2>4. Upper Body Strength Foundation</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: general training principle. A dead hang is an isometric hold that involves the lats, posterior deltoids, trapezius, rhomboids, forearm flexors and trunk muscles.</p>

